# Ultrasound Signal Conversion: Time Domain to Frequency Domain

Here's a complete MATLAB program to convert an ultrasound signal from voltage vs. time to magnitude (dB) vs. frequency (MHz):

```matlab
%% Ultrasound Signal: Time Domain to Frequency Domain Conversion
% Converts Voltage vs Time signal to Magnitude (dB) vs Frequency (MHz)

clear; clc; close all;

%% 1. Load or Generate Ultrasound Signal
% Option A: Load from file (uncomment if you have your own data)
% data = load('ultrasound_data.txt');   % or .csv, .mat
% t = data(:,1);                         % Time vector (seconds)
% v = data(:,2);                         % Voltage vector (Volts)

% Option B: Generate a synthetic ultrasound signal for demonstration
Fs = 100e6;                  % Sampling frequency: 100 MHz
dt = 1/Fs;                   % Time step
T  = 5e-6;                   % Total duration: 5 microseconds
t  = (0:dt:T-dt)';           % Time vector

% Simulated ultrasound pulse (5 MHz Gaussian-modulated sinusoid)
fc = 5e6;                    % Center frequency: 5 MHz
bw = 0.6;                    % Fractional bandwidth
tc = T/3;                    % Pulse center time
sigma = 1/(2*pi*fc*bw/2);    % Gaussian envelope width
v = exp(-((t-tc).^2)/(2*sigma^2)) .* sin(2*pi*fc*(t-tc));

% Add some noise (optional)
v = v + 0.02*randn(size(v));

%% 2. Pre-processing
v = v - mean(v);             % Remove DC offset
N = length(v);               % Number of samples

% Apply a window to reduce spectral leakage (Hann window)
w = hann(N);
v_windowed = v .* w;

%% 3. Compute FFT
NFFT = 2^nextpow2(N);        % Zero-pad to next power of 2 (for speed/resolution)
V = fft(v_windowed, NFFT);   % Compute FFT

% Single-sided spectrum
V_mag = abs(V/N);                       % Normalized magnitude
V_single = V_mag(1:NFFT/2+1);
V_single(2:end-1) = 2*V_single(2:end-1);% Compensate for one-sided

% Frequency vector
f = Fs*(0:(NFFT/2))/NFFT;    % In Hz
f_MHz = f/1e6;               % Convert to MHz

% Convert magnitude to dB (reference = max value for normalization)
V_dB = 20*log10(V_single / max(V_single) + eps);

%% 4. Plot Results
figure('Color','w','Position',[100 100 900 600]);

% Time-domain plot
subplot(2,1,1);
plot(t*1e6, v, 'b','LineWidth',1.2);
xlabel('Time (\mus)');
ylabel('Voltage (V)');
title('Ultrasound Signal — Time Domain');
grid on; xlim([0 T*1e6]);

% Frequency-domain plot (dB vs MHz)
subplot(2,1,2);
plot(f_MHz, V_dB, 'r','LineWidth',1.2);
xlabel('Frequency (MHz)');
ylabel('Magnitude (dB)');
title('Ultrasound Signal — Frequency Domain');
grid on;
xlim([0 Fs/2/1e6]);          % up to Nyquist
ylim([-80 5]);

%% 5. Find and Report Key Parameters
[peak_dB, idx] = max(V_dB);
peak_freq = f_MHz(idx);

% -6 dB Bandwidth
above_6dB = find(V_dB >= -6);
if ~isempty(above_6dB)
    BW_6dB = f_MHz(above_6dB(end)) - f_MHz(above_6dB(1));
else
    BW_6dB = NaN;
end

fprintf('--------- Signal Analysis ---------\n');
fprintf('Sampling Frequency : %.2f MHz\n', Fs/1e6);
fprintf('Signal Duration    : %.2f us\n', T*1e6);
fprintf('Peak Frequency     : %.3f MHz\n', peak_freq);
fprintf('-6 dB Bandwidth    : %.3f MHz\n', BW_6dB);
fprintf('-----------------------------------\n');
```

## Key Features

| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| **DC removal** | Eliminates baseline offset before FFT |
| **Hann window** | Reduces spectral leakage |
| **Zero-padding** | Improves frequency resolution |
| **One-sided spectrum** | Physical positive frequencies only |
| **dB normalization** | Magnitude referenced to peak (0 dB at max) |
| **Bandwidth calculation** | Reports the -6 dB bandwidth |

## How to Use With Your Own Data

Replace the signal generation section with:

```matlab
data = readmatrix('your_data.csv');   % or load('your_data.mat')
t = data(:,1);
v = data(:,2);
Fs = 1/mean(diff(t));                 % Auto-detect sampling rate
```

## Tips
- If your time vector is in **µs**, convert to seconds: `t = t * 1e-6;`
- For non-uniform sampling, interpolate first using `interp1` onto a uniform grid.
- To reference dB to 1 V (dBV) instead of peak, use `V_dB = 20*log10(V_single);`

